{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793691602,"nonce":"qyifskRi+XgonyNoTlbH6JESax8=","title":"Language benchmark pages keep one tab per CI machine","message":"Each run of wiki.yml lands on whichever CPUs the host hands it, and the zylua and zypy pages showed only the latest run's single Linux and single macOS machine, so the same code read as 20/22 against PyPy on one run and 14/22 on the next. Over the last 14 runs the Python job ran on EPYC 7763, 9V74, 9V45 and Intel Xeon Platinum 8573C and Xeon 6973P-C, and the Lua job on EPYC 7763 and 9V74, Xeon 8573C and Xeon 6975P-C.\n\nEach page now reads machines/index.json, built at deploy time by .github/scripts/carry_machines.py: it downloads the machine files the previous deploy published, replaces the machines this run measured, and writes the index. The pages build a tab per machine and open on the one measured last. A deploy that cannot read the earlier machines stops, so it cannot publish a page that has lost them. Without an index the pages read results.json and results-macos.json as before.\n\nNot done: the ZynML kernel page (its per-kernel jobs can each land on a different machine, so a result file there mixes CPUs); keeping several runs per machine and showing their median, which would take the run-to-run variance out of the numbers (zypy varies 1.25x geomean between runs on one EPYC model, PyPy 1.06x). Unverified in CI until the first deploy after the push: check that machines/index.json lists the machines and that a second deploy keeps them.","files":null}]}